(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-quiz"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which is first-line drug therapy for symptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Adenosine","Beta blocker","Intravenous magnesium","Digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is treated first with a beta blocker such as metoprolol or carvedilol. If that is not effective or not tolerated, catheter ablation is the primary alternative, and amiodarone, lidocaine or procainamide come after that. Asymptomatic runs only need optimized treatment of heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What separates pulseless electrical activity from asystole?","choices":["An organized rhythm, no pulse","A flat line, no activity","Chaotic waves, no pulse","A wide rhythm with a pulse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless electrical activity shows an electrocardiogram on which you would expect a pulse, such as normal sinus rhythm, yet there are no perfusing beats. Asystole has no rate, rhythm, P waves or QRS complexes at all. Both are unshockable and get compressions and epinephrine as soon as possible. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which premature ventricular contraction burden puts a patient in the high-risk group?","choices":["Any single ectopic","Over 0.5% of beats","Over 1% of beats","Over 15% of beats"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A burden over 15% of beats, or 15,000 a day, is a high-risk feature, along with premature-beat cardiomyopathy, pre-existing structural or conduction disease and a history of syncope. High-risk patients get a beta blocker with optimized cardiac care, or ablation for isolated cardiomyopathy with heart failure signs. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Low calcium, high sodium","High phosphate, low chloride","Low potassium, low magnesium","High potassium, low sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, along with hypercalcemia, are the electrolyte imbalances named as causes of premature ventricular contractions, together with hypoxemia, alcohol, caffeine and stimulants. These beats occur with or without structural heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which is the first treatment for torsades de pointes in a conscious patient?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium is given to break torsades de pointes, then more magnesium as an infusion. Amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided because they prolong the QT; if the patient becomes hemodynamically unstable, defibrillation replaces drug therapy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block, which symptom is associated with an increased risk of sudden death?","choices":["Syncope","Fatigue","Palpitations","Ankle swelling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of complete block range from none to severe, depending on the ventricular escape rate, the site of the escape rhythm and underlying heart disease. Syncope is singled out because it is associated with an increased risk of sudden death, and the block is treated definitively with a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia treated, even when the patient has a pulse?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous lidocaine alone","Observation on a monitor","Defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Even when the patient has a pulse and looks well, polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable; it is a short-lived transitional state. It is defibrillated, followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization, and intravenous metoprolol or amiodarone may be added. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In an elderly patient, which cause tops the list for both Mobitz type II and complete atrioventricular block?","choices":["Recent Lyme infection","Inherited long QT","His-Purkinje degeneration","Increased vagal tone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acquired disease of the His-Purkinje and conduction system is the most common cause of both Mobitz type II and complete block, especially degenerative change from old age, chronic hypertension and diabetes. Both blocks are treated definitively with a permanent pacemaker, with transcutaneous pacing as the emergent bridge. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 13, 18"},{"q":"Which procedure is a named cause of Mobitz type I block?","choices":["Pericardiocentesis","Transthoracic echocardiography","Cardiac ablation","Coronary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac ablation is a named cause of Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block, along with increased vagal tone, beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, digoxin, Lyme carditis and atrioventricular node ischemia after infarction. The block is usually asymptomatic and needs no treatment unless symptoms develop. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which is the most common cause of first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["Overactive vagal tone","Age-related conduction degeneration","Lyme disease of the heart","Atrioventricular node ischemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is the most common cause of first-degree block, a constant PR interval over 0.20 seconds. Other causes are vagal tone, beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, digoxin, hypokalemia, nodal ischemia, chronic hypertension and Lyme disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which two arrest rhythms are shockable?","choices":["Fibrillation, pulseless tachycardia","Asystole, fibrillation","Pulseless electrical, asystole","Tachycardia, pulseless electrical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia are the shockable arrest rhythms: they are defibrillated as soon as possible, followed by 2 minutes of cardiopulmonary resuscitation, epinephrine and, if still shockable, amiodarone. Asystole and pulseless electrical activity are unshockable. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 50, 55"},{"q":"For an adult with rare premature ventricular contractions, a normal heart and no symptoms, which care strategy fits?","choices":["Implanted defibrillator","Reassurance, avoid triggers","Beta blocker for life","Ablation this month"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rare premature ventricular contractions (a low burden, under 1% or 1,000 a day), a normal heart and no symptoms put this adult in the lowest-risk group, so the plan is reassurance and eliminating triggers such as alcohol, caffeine and stimulants. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which chronic pulmonary vascular condition is a named cause of right bundle branch block?","choices":["Pulmonary hypertension","Pleural effusion","Obstructive sleep apnea","Solitary pulmonary nodule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is a named chronic cause of right bundle branch block, alongside age-related degeneration, chronic ischemic heart disease, a sudden rise in right ventricular pressure from pulmonary embolism, cardiomyopathies and inflammatory or infiltrative disease. Treatment depends on that underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is a Stokes-Adams attack?","choices":["Palpitations felt pounding in the neck","Exertional chest pain relieved by rest","Seizure caused by low blood glucose","Sudden faint from low brain blood flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Stokes-Adams attack is a sudden fainting spell or seizure from a rapid, temporary drop in blood flow to the brain. Mobitz type II can lead to it, because its block usually sits at the bundle of His and leaves a weak, unreliable escape rhythm; the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which chronic condition is a named cause of left bundle branch block?","choices":["Chronic pulmonary hypertension","Chronic hepatitis","Chronic migraine","Chronic hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypertension is a named cause of left bundle branch block, alongside chronic ischemic heart disease and severe coronary artery disease, dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy, valvular disease (especially aortic stenosis) and Lenègre-Lev disease. Most patients are asymptomatic, and prognosis depends on the underlying disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which parasitic infection is a named cause of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Giardiasis","Chagas disease","Malaria","Toxoplasmosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chagas disease is a named cause of third-degree (complete) block, alongside degenerative conduction disease, Lyme carditis, bacterial endocarditis with an abscess, inflammatory and infiltrative disease, myocardial infarction and ablation. Complete block is paced emergently, then treated with a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"For an elderly patient with Mobitz type II block found incidentally and causing no symptoms, which plan is appropriate?","choices":["Start a beta blocker","Permanent pacemaker","Reassure and observe","Recheck in a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Even though this elderly patient has no symptoms, Mobitz type II is considered dangerous because of its unpredictable progression to complete heart block and sudden cardiac death, and its escape rhythm is weak and unreliable. The definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which history places a patient with premature ventricular contractions in the high-risk group?","choices":["Age under 30","Occasional anxiety","Prior syncope","Daily caffeine use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A history of syncope places a patient with premature ventricular contractions in the high-risk group, along with a burden over 15% (15,000 a day), premature-beat cardiomyopathy and pre-existing structural or conduction disease. High-risk patients start a beta blocker and optimized cardiac care. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why are amiodarone and procainamide avoided in torsades de pointes?","choices":["They prolong the QT interval","They lower serum magnesium","They shorten the PR interval","They cause severe hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Torsades de pointes is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with QT prolongation, so amiodarone and procainamide, which prolong the QT, are avoided. Beta blockers are avoided too, since bradycardia lengthens the R-to-R interval; magnesium is the treatment and lidocaine the one usable antiarrhythmic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is ventricular bigeminy?","choices":["Every third beat is ectopic","PR lengthens, then drops","Three ectopics in a row","Every other beat is ectopic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When every other beat is an ectopic beat, the pattern is bigeminy; when every third beat is ectopic, it is trigeminy. In ventricular bigeminy each ectopic beat is early, with a wide, bizarre QRS complex and no P wave. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram pattern defines Mobitz type II block?","choices":["Long constant PR, no dropped beats","Constant PR with random dropped QRS","PR lengthens until a QRS drops","P waves unrelated to the QRS"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II appears regular apart from random missing QRS complexes, so there are more P waves than QRS complexes. The PR interval of the conducted beats stays constant at 0.12 to 0.20 seconds, and the QRS is narrow, under 0.12 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which is the management of asymptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Lifelong oral amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Treat the underlying disease","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is managed by optimizing treatment of the underlying cardiac comorbidities. Drug therapy starts only when runs cause symptoms, beginning with a beta blocker, and the runs themselves are not an indication for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD). — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why are beta blockers avoided in drug-induced torsades de pointes?","choices":["They cause hypokalemia","They shorten the QT too much","They interact with magnesium","Bradycardia lengthens the QT"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers cause bradycardia and lengthen the R-to-R interval, which indirectly prolongs the QT and worsens drug-induced torsades de pointes. Amiodarone and procainamide are avoided for direct QT prolongation; intravenous magnesium is the treatment and lidocaine is the one usable antiarrhythmic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which statement about shocking asystole and pulseless electrical activity is accurate?","choices":["Both need defibrillation","Shock if the line is flat","Shock if the rhythm is organized","Both are unshockable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless electrical activity and asystole are both unshockable. Chest compressions start immediately, early intubation is considered, and high-quality compressions with epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes are the mainstays. Both carry a poor prognosis; hypovolemia and hypoxia are the two leading causes of pulseless electrical activity. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which external device detects and shocks fatal ventricular arrhythmias while worn on the body?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing pads","Wearable defibrillator vest","Implantable loop recorder","Ambulatory Holter monitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A wearable (external) cardioverter-defibrillator, the LifeVest, is worn on the body and, like an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD), detects fatal arrhythmias and delivers a shock to prevent sudden cardiac death. The implanted version is the alternative, and newer implanted devices can also pace. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Above which PR interval do symptoms of first-degree atrioventricular block usually begin to appear?","choices":["0.12 seconds","0.20 seconds","0.3 seconds","0.6 seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The vast majority of first-degree blocks are asymptomatic. Symptoms such as fatigue, shortness of breath, palpitations and chest pain usually appear once the PR interval exceeds 0.3 seconds, and a permanent pacemaker is considered for symptomatic patients or a PR above 0.3 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of asystole and pulseless electrical activity?","choices":["Excellent with shocks","Good if found early","Poor; usually end-of-life","Better than fibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asystole and pulseless electrical activity carry a poor prognosis and are usually end-of-life rhythms. They are unshockable and are treated with immediate high-quality compressions and epinephrine; ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation have the best prognosis of the arrest rhythms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death?","choices":["An accessory pathway","Coronary artery disease","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Long QT syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coronary artery disease is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death, even in patients with no history of myocardial infarction. Sudden cardiac death is unexpected cardiac death within 1 hour of collapse, from ventricular fibrillation, pulseless ventricular tachycardia, pulseless electrical activity or asystole. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When ventricular fibrillation stays refractory to shocks after an antiarrhythmic, which drug may be considered?","choices":["Verapamil","Metoprolol","Adenosine","Lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In ventricular fibrillation, defibrillation, cardiopulmonary resuscitation and epinephrine come first, then amiodarone if the rhythm persists after a second shock. A lidocaine push may be considered when the rhythm stays refractory to shock after that antiarrhythmic, along with calcium gluconate or sodium bicarbonate. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Once stabilized after unstable ventricular tachycardia, what do most patients need?","choices":["An implantable defibrillator","A permanent pacemaker","Lifelong oral magnesium","No further treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized after unstable ventricular tachycardia, most patients need an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death related to life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. The device senses fatal arrhythmias and delivers a shock. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"}]};
